Let tg3 probe when the board keeps its address elsewhere.

Arista keeps a switch's MAC in board data on an i2c SEEPROM, not in the NIC.
A stock tg3 aborts the probe and the machine has no management interface at
all:

    tg3 0000:04:00.0: Could not obtain valid ethernet address, aborting
    tg3 0000:04:00.0: probe with driver tg3 failed with error -2

Two paths reach that, and the second is the one this hardware takes. The NVRAM
does not hold nothing -- it holds Broadcom's default address, which is valid
enough to pass is_valid_ether_addr. The driver then asks the device tree or
ACPI for the real one, finds nothing there either on a board that keeps it in
its own i2c data, and returns that error. Patching only the invalid-address
path leaves the abort exactly as it was, which is what the first attempt here
did.

A random address lets the device exist. It is deliberately not the right one:
the board's real address is applied from its configuration afterwards, and that
cannot happen while there is no interface to apply it to. Both fallbacks warn,
because a machine that silently invents a MAC is one that moves on the network
for reasons nobody can see.

This is a stopgap. Reading the address from the board's own prefdl is the real
fix and belongs in board support, not in this driver.

Note for anyone editing this file: no line of this preamble may begin with a
plus or minus, or patch reads it as diff content.

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/broadcom/tg3.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/broadcom/tg3.c
@@ -17091,11 +17091,42 @@
 		}
 	}
 
-	if (!is_valid_ether_addr(addr))
-		return -EINVAL;
+	if (!is_valid_ether_addr(addr)) {
+		/*
+		 * NOSaic: some boards do not keep the address anywhere this
+		 * driver looks. Arista keeps it in board data on an i2c
+		 * SEEPROM, so a stock probe finds nothing and aborts, and the
+		 * machine comes up with no management interface at all.
+		 *
+		 * A random address lets the device probe. It is not the right
+		 * address and is not meant to be: the board's real one is
+		 * applied from its configuration once the interface exists,
+		 * which cannot happen if there is no interface. Announced
+		 * loudly, because an unannounced random MAC is a machine that
+		 * moves on the network for no visible reason.
+		 */
+		eth_random_addr(addr);
+		dev_warn(&tp->pdev->dev,
+			 "no address in NVRAM; using a random one until the board supplies its own\n");
+		return 0;
+	}
 
-	if (tg3_is_default_mac_address(addr))
-		return device_get_mac_address(&tp->pdev->dev, addr);
+	if (tg3_is_default_mac_address(addr)) {
+		/*
+		 * The NVRAM holds Broadcom's default rather than this board's
+		 * address, so the driver asks the device tree or ACPI for one.
+		 * On a board that keeps its address in its own i2c board data
+		 * there is nothing there either, and the error from here is
+		 * what aborts the probe -- which is the path this hardware
+		 * actually takes, not the invalid-address one above.
+		 */
+		if (device_get_mac_address(&tp->pdev->dev, addr)) {
+			eth_random_addr(addr);
+			dev_warn(&tp->pdev->dev,
+				 "only a default address available; using a random one until the board supplies its own\n");
+		}
+		return 0;
+	}
 
 	return 0;
 }
